{% extends "_layouts/default.njk" %}

{% set pageName = "How we display ages and intervals" %}
{% set headerOptions = {
  service: {
    text: serviceName
  }
} %}

{% block content %}
  <!-- Discussion artefact, not a product page. All values are hard-coded and were checked with a throwaway script. -->
  <div class="nhsuk-grid-row">
    <div class="nhsuk-grid-column-two-thirds">
      <h1 class="nhsuk-heading-xl">
        <span class="nhsuk-caption-xl">For internal discussion</span>
        How we display ages and intervals
      </h1>

      <p class="nhsuk-lede-text">A displayed age such as &ldquo;11 months&rdquo; can mean several different things. This page sets out the problem, works through examples and lists ways we could display ages and intervals.</p>

      <h2 class="nhsuk-heading-l" id="the-problem">The problem</h2>

      <p>When we show an age or an interval, we usually show fewer units than we know. &ldquo;11 months&rdquo; leaves out the days.</p>

      <p>That means the displayed value is often a range. The reader cannot tell which of these it is:</p>

      <ul class="nhsuk-list nhsuk-list--bullet">
        <li><strong>truncated</strong>: the extra days were dropped, so &ldquo;11 months&rdquo; is anything from 11 months 0 days up to the day before 12 months</li>
        <li><strong>exact</strong>: the child was exactly 11 months old, with 0 days left over</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Both give the same label. An exact age with a zero left out looks the same as an age with days dropped. &ldquo;11 months&rdquo; and &ldquo;11 months 0 days&rdquo; can describe the same child on the same day, but only the second one tells the reader that days were counted.</p>

      <p>To read the first one correctly, the reader has to know how precise the system is. Nothing on the screen tells them.</p>

      <p>For most reading this does not matter. It does matter when a clinician uses the age to decide whether a dose was valid.</p>

      <h3 class="nhsuk-heading-m">An example from this prototype</h3>

      <p>The prototype&rsquo;s age at vaccination text leaves out zero units, and drops days entirely from 1 year upwards. For a child born on 15 March 2025, it shows:</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <table class="nhsuk-table-responsive" role="table">
