  <div class="nhsuk-grid-row">
    <div class="nhsuk-grid-column-two-thirds">
      <p>What the examples show:</p>

      <ul class="nhsuk-list nhsuk-list--bullet">
        <li>Truncation hides how close each dose was. The doses on 25 February, 5 March and 14 March all show as &ldquo;11 months&rdquo;, although they were 18, 10 and 1 day early.</li>
        <li>Truncation also hides closeness on the valid side. &ldquo;1 year&rdquo; for 15 March does not tell the reader the dose was given on the birthday itself.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="nhsuk-heading-l" id="calendar-edge-cases">Calendar edge cases</h2>

      <p>Months have different lengths, so adding or counting months is not always well defined.</p>

      <h3 class="nhsuk-heading-m">Born 31 January 2025, date 28 February 2025</h3>

      <p>Is the child 0 months 28 days old, or 1 month 0 days old?</p>

      <ul class="nhsuk-list nhsuk-list--bullet">
        <li>If a month is complete only when the same day number is reached, there is no 31 February, so the child is 0 months 28 days.</li>
        <li>If the anniversary moves to the last day of a short month, 28 February counts as the 1-month anniversary, so the child is 1 month 0 days.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="nhsuk-heading-m">Born 29 February 2024, date 28 February 2025</h3>

      <p>Is the child 0 years 11 months 30 days old, or 1 year 0 months 0 days old?</p>

      <ul class="nhsuk-list nhsuk-list--bullet">
        <li>If the birthday must fall on the same day number, there is no 29 February in 2025, so the child is 0 years 11 months 30 days.</li>
        <li>If the birthday moves to the last day of February, the child turned 1 on 28 February, so they are 1 year 0 months 0 days.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Systems and libraries answer these questions differently. In a quick check, date-fns (the date library used in this prototype) gave 1 month 0 days for the first example and &ldquo;0 years 12 months 0 days&rdquo; for the second. Ruby&rsquo;s month addition moves both anniversaries to 28 February.</p>

      <div class="nhsuk-inset-text">
        <span class="nhsuk-u-visually-hidden">Information: </span>
        <p>Formatting alone does not resolve this. Choosing how to display an age does not decide how the age is calculated. The calculation rule has to be agreed separately.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="nhsuk-heading-l" id="options">Options</h2>

      <p>Each option shows how the MMR example would look for the invalid dose on 14 March 2026 and the valid dose on 15 March 2026. The options are listed in no order of preference.</p>

      <div class="nhsuk-card">
        <div class="nhsuk-card__content">
          <h3 class="nhsuk-card__heading">1. Largest unit only, truncated</h3>
          <p class="nhsuk-body-s nhsuk-u-secondary-text-colour">Current common practice.</p>
          <ul class="nhsuk-list">
            <li>14 March 2026: <strong>11 months</strong></li>
            <li>15 March 2026: <strong>1 year</strong></li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="nhsuk-heading-s">Helps</h4>
          <ul class="nhsuk-list nhsuk-list--bullet">
            <li>short enough to fit narrow table cells and scan quickly down a column</li>
            <li>familiar, and matches how people talk about a child&rsquo;s age</li>
            <li>reads naturally with a screen reader</li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="nhsuk-heading-s">Risks</h4>
          <ul class="nhsuk-list nhsuk-list--bullet">
            <li>does not answer &ldquo;is this dose valid?&rdquo; near a threshold without opening the dates</li>
            <li>&ldquo;11 months&rdquo; for a dose 1 day early looks the same as one 27 days earlier</li>
            <li>&ldquo;1 year&rdquo; can span a whole year of ages, so comparing across records says little</li>
            <li>the reader cannot tell whether the value is exact or has days dropped, because a zero left out looks the same as days dropped</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="nhsuk-card">
        <div class="nhsuk-card__content">
          <h3 class="nhsuk-card__heading">2. Always full years, months and days</h3>
          <ul class="nhsuk-list">
            <li>14 March 2026: <strong>0 years 11 months 27 days</strong></li>
            <li>15 March 2026: <strong>1 year 0 months 0 days</strong></li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="nhsuk-heading-s">Helps</h4>
          <ul class="nhsuk-list nhsuk-list--bullet">
            <li>no hidden range, because nothing is dropped</li>
            <li>zeros are shown, so the reader can see the precision: &ldquo;11 months 0 days&rdquo; says days were counted</li>
            <li>the 1 day gap between a valid and invalid dose is visible</li>
            <li>same shape on every record, so values line up when compared</li>
            <li>reads clearly with a screen reader</li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="nhsuk-heading-s">Risks</h4>
          <ul class="nhsuk-list nhsuk-list--bullet">
            <li>long, around 25 characters, which wraps in table cells</li>
            <li>slower to scan, and the important part can be lost among zeros</li>
            <li>precision that most readers do not need most of the time</li>
            <li>still depends on an agreed month-end rule</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="nhsuk-card">
        <div class="nhsuk-card__content">
          <h3 class="nhsuk-card__heading">3. Compact full form</h3>
          <ul class="nhsuk-list">
            <li>14 March 2026: <strong>0y 11m 27d</strong></li>
            <li>15 March 2026: <strong>1y 0m 0d</strong></li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="nhsuk-heading-s">Helps</h4>
          <ul class="nhsuk-list nhsuk-list--bullet">
            <li>full precision in about 10 characters, so it fits table cells</li>
            <li>zeros are shown, so &ldquo;11m 0d&rdquo; cannot be mistaken for an age with days dropped</li>
            <li>fixed shape makes values easy to compare down a column</li>
            <li>answers &ldquo;is this dose valid?&rdquo; at a glance for readers who know the format</li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="nhsuk-heading-s">Risks</h4>
          <ul class="nhsuk-list nhsuk-list--bullet">
            <li>screen readers may read &ldquo;11m&rdquo; as &ldquo;11 metres&rdquo; or &ldquo;11 minutes&rdquo;, or spell out letters</li>
            <li>abbreviations are harder for less frequent users and may go against NHS style</li>
            <li>&ldquo;m&rdquo; could be misread as minutes in contexts that also show times</li>
            <li>still depends on an agreed month-end rule</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="nhsuk-card">
        <div class="nhsuk-card__content">
          <h3 class="nhsuk-card__heading">4. Precision that changes with age</h3>
          <p class="nhsuk-body-s nhsuk-u-secondary-text-colour">Based on the archived NHS Common User Interface patient banner guidance (ISB 1505). To confirm against the archived CUI source.</p>
          <p>Weeks and days under 1 year, months and days under 2 years, years and months under 18, and years only after that.</p>
          <ul class="nhsuk-list">
            <li>14 March 2026: <strong>52 weeks 0 days</strong></li>
            <li>15 March 2026: <strong>12 months 0 days</strong></li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="nhsuk-heading-s">Helps</h4>
          <ul class="nhsuk-list nhsuk-list--bullet">
            <li>has an NHS precedent, so may be familiar to some clinicians</li>
            <li>gives day-level precision in the age ranges where schedules are densest</li>
            <li>stays short for older children and adults</li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="nhsuk-heading-s">Risks</h4>
          <ul class="nhsuk-list nhsuk-list--bullet">
            <li>the unit changes at the threshold, so the invalid and valid doses look like different kinds of value</li>
            <li>&ldquo;52 weeks&rdquo; has to be converted mentally before it answers &ldquo;is this dose valid?&rdquo;</li>
            <li>a column of mixed units is hard to compare across records</li>
            <li>from 2 to 18 years it drops days, so the range problem returns for school-age thresholds</li>
            <li>the reader has to know which precision applies at each age to know what was dropped, and nothing on screen tells them</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="nhsuk-card">
        <div class="nhsuk-card__content">
          <h3 class="nhsuk-card__heading">5. Extra precision only near a clinical threshold</h3>
          <p>Show the short form normally, and add days when the value falls within a set window of a threshold, for example within 1 month.</p>
          <ul class="nhsuk-list">
            <li>14 March 2026: <strong>11 months 27 days</strong></li>
            <li>15 March 2026: <strong>1 year 0 months 0 days</strong></li>
            <li>20 April 2026 (outside the window): <strong>1 year 1 month</strong></li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="nhsuk-heading-s">Helps</h4>
          <ul class="nhsuk-list nhsuk-list--bullet">
            <li>short where precision does not matter, precise where it does</li>
            <li>the extra length itself signals &ldquo;look closely&rdquo;</li>
            <li>answers &ldquo;is this dose valid?&rdquo; in the cases that are close</li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="nhsuk-heading-s">Risks</h4>
          <ul class="nhsuk-list nhsuk-list--bullet">
            <li>needs every threshold, for every programme and dose, encoded in the display logic</li>
            <li>the window size is arbitrary, and a value just outside it is still a hidden range</li>
            <li>outside the window, &ldquo;11 months&rdquo; could be exact or have days dropped, and the reader cannot tell whether the value was inside the window</li>
            <li>inconsistent formats in one column make comparison harder</li>
            <li>readers may not know why some values have days and others do not</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="nhsuk-card">
        <div class="nhsuk-card__content">
          <h3 class="nhsuk-card__heading">6. Relative to the threshold</h3>
          <ul class="nhsuk-list">
            <li>14 March 2026: <strong>Given 1 day before first birthday</strong></li>
            <li>15 March 2026: <strong>Given on first birthday</strong></li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="nhsuk-heading-s">Helps</h4>
          <ul class="nhsuk-list nhsuk-list--bullet">
            <li>answers &ldquo;is this dose valid?&rdquo; directly, with no arithmetic</li>
            <li>plain language that reads well with a screen reader</li>
            <li>avoids most month-end questions, because it counts days to a single date</li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="nhsuk-heading-s">Risks</h4>
          <ul class="nhsuk-list nhsuk-list--bullet">
            <li>only works where there is one clear threshold for that dose</li>
            <li>does not tell the reader the child&rsquo;s actual age</li>
            <li>long sentences in table cells, and harder to scan down a column</li>
            <li>moves a clinical rule into display text, so the rule itself must be agreed and kept up to date</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="nhsuk-card">
        <div class="nhsuk-card__content">
          <h3 class="nhsuk-card__heading">7. Dates first</h3>
          <p>Show the date of birth and the vaccination date, with the derived age as secondary text.</p>
          <ul class="nhsuk-list">
            <li>14 March 2026: <strong>Born 15 March 2025, given 14 March 2026</strong> <span class="nhsuk-u-secondary-text-colour">(age 11 months 27 days)</span></li>
            <li>15 March 2026: <strong>Born 15 March 2025, given 15 March 2026</strong> <span class="nhsuk-u-secondary-text-colour">(age 1 year 0 months 0 days)</span></li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="nhsuk-heading-s">Helps</h4>
          <ul class="nhsuk-list nhsuk-list--bullet">
            <li>the source facts are always visible, so nothing is hidden by the display rule</li>
            <li>clinicians can check validity themselves when the derived age is in doubt</li>
            <li>the derived age can change convention without changing the facts shown</li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="nhsuk-heading-s">Risks</h4>
          <ul class="nhsuk-list nhsuk-list--bullet">
            <li>comparing 2 dates is slower than reading one age, especially across many rows</li>
            <li>widest option, and hard to fit in table cells</li>
            <li>screen readers read 2 full dates before the age</li>
            <li>the secondary age still needs a convention of its own</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="nhsuk-heading-l" id="open-questions">Open questions</h2>

      <ul class="nhsuk-list nhsuk-list--bullet">
        <li>Mavis truncates ages. Which library or calculation does it use, and does it truncate the same way everywhere an age is shown?</li>
        <li>How is month-end arithmetic resolved, for example a child born on 31 January or 29 February?</li>
        <li>Do age at vaccination and current age need different conventions?</li>
        <li>Do screen readers read &ldquo;0y 11m 27d&rdquo; sensibly?</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
{% endblock %}
